    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Strict border enforcement can disrupt specific economic mechanisms, most notably by increasing the cost of living for essential goods like fuel or natural gas when it creates a key \"fence post\" in the geopolitical \"inability to trade\" for certain agricultural and imported fuel products. For instance, if aggressive policies block the free flow of specific agricultural goods that the US relies on for domestic food security, this can lead to immediate price spikes and supply chain inefficiencies for large sectors.\n\nFurthermore, the long-term economic stability relies on a certain degree of labor mobility; if enforcement is so strict that it completely sever ties between necessary worker shortages in the US (like in construction or agriculture) and the available labor force from other nations, it can stifle productivity and growth. Ultimately, while sovereignty is paramount, these rigid enforcement measures can inadvertently create economic bottlenecks that require policy adjustments to ensure balanced national development.",
      "source": "target",
