      "content": "i see. so you're saying that sovereignty might be \"paramount\" but these policies still cause problems. though, if we're talking about national sovereignty, isn't a country's primary duty to actually secure its borders regardless of the economic cost? it feels like if you can't control who enters, you've already lost sovereignty. wouldn't a policy that prioritizes security over these \"economic bottlenecks\" actually be more effective for the nation in the long run?",
